Approach Documentation
-------------------------------------
How I Approached This Assignment
-------------------------------------
Step 1: Infrastructure First
    - Provisioned all AWS resources using Terraform before writing any pipeline code.
    - This ensures the deployment target exists before automating deployments.

Step 2: Happy Path First
    - Followed the assignment's advice — got the basic flow working end-to-end
    - before adding security scanning, notifications, and edge cases.

Step 3: Cost Consciousness
 All tools used in the project was evaluated for cost:
    - Free tier AWS resources only (t2.micro, db.t3.micro)
    - Open source monitoring (Prometheus/Grafana) over paid SaaS
    - GitHub Container Registry over ECR to avoid registry costs
    - Single NAT Gateway over per-AZ to save ~$30/month

Step 4: Security by Default
    - Private subnets for database
    - Security groups with least-privilege rules
    - Container and dependency scanning on every build
    - Secrets never in code — always in GitHub Secrets

-------------------------------
Tool Choices Explained
-------------------------------

A. Terraform over CloudFormation
   Terraform is cloud-agnostic and industry standard. Skills transfer across clouds.
   CloudFormation is AWS-specific and less portable.

B. FastAPI over Django/Flask
   FastAPI is modern, fast, and has built-in OpenAPI documentation.
   The template also came with Docker, tests, and PostgreSQL already configured.

C. GitHub Actions over Jenkins
   No infrastructure to manage. Native GitHub integration.
   Free for public repositories. YAML-based, easy to read and maintain.

D. Prometheus + Grafana over CloudWatch
   Zero cost. More flexible. Industry standard for Kubernetes and container monitoring.
   Dashboards are portable and can be version controlled as JSON.